% ============================================================================
% solfege — each movable-do solfège syllable's scale degree
% (adj-facts-stdlib, MUSIC).
% ============================================================================
% "Do, a deer, a female deer…" — solfège gives the seven notes of the major scale
% singable NAMES: do comes first (scale degree 1), re next (2), … all the way to
% ti, the seventh. In the MOVABLE-DO system these syllables track the note's
% FUNCTION in the scale (do is always the tonic, wherever the key sits), so the
% degree number is what a musician means when they say "the fifth is sol" or
% "resolve ti up to do". This tiny library records that syllable → degree mapping
% so an ADJ program can ASK where a syllable sits in the scale, and get an answer
% with its evidence attached — cited outright for two rows, and DISCLOSED AS
% COMPOSED for the other five (see Provenance below, and note that the degree
% NAMES used in this paragraph and the next are ordinary music theory, not
% something either citation states). Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? solfege_degree(do, $N)       % 1  (do is the tonic, the first degree)
%     ? solfege_degree(sol, $N)      % 5  (sol is the fifth — the dominant)
%     ? solfege_degree(ti, $N)       % 7  (ti is the leading tone, the seventh)
%
% Because the value is a NUMBER, the answer composes into arithmetic — how many
% steps from mi up to la? 6 − 3 = 3. A reverse query binds the other column,
% turning a degree back into its syllable:
%
%     ? solfege_degree($S, 3)        % mi  (which syllable is the third degree?)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `solfege_degree(syllable, degree)` carrying the table's citation, so
% every lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% degree (or the syllable, on a reverse query) WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and ABSTAINS on anything that is not one of the seven
% syllables (a random word, a number) rather than guessing.
%
% The seven syllables, as an ordered truth table:
%
%     syllable   degree      name of the degree
%     --------   ------      ------------------
%     do         1           tonic
%     re         2           supertonic
%     mi         3           mediant
%     fa         4           subdominant
%     sol        5           dominant
%     la         6           submediant
%     ti         7           leading tone
%
% The third column is the conventional NAME of each scale degree. It is NOT part
% of the shipped table — no row carries it, and neither cited span states it. It
% is here only to explain what a degree number means to a reader.
%
% Only the seven diatonic-scale rows are shipped — nothing else. A chromatic
% syllable (the raised `di`/`ri`, the lowered `te`/`le`) has NO row, and neither
% does a non-syllable, so a query like `? solfege_degree(di, $N)` ABSTAINS rather
% than inventing a degree: the table only ever asserts what it can cite, or what
% it discloses as composed from what it can cite — see Provenance.
%
% Provenance — nothing here is from memory, and the two cited spans do NOT ground
% the seven rows equally, so this note says exactly which rows each one carries.
% The `source` is the Wikipedia "Solfège" article's movable-do sentence, which
% states the mapping outright — "each solfège syllable corresponds not to a
% pitch, but to a scale degree" — and then names the syllable sung for the first
% degree and for the second, closing with "etc." That grounds (do, 1) and
% (re, 2) VERBATIM, and the "etc." licenses the continuation without spelling it
% out. (The sentence's own quotation marks around the two syllables are why this
% note does not re-quote it whole: reproducing a value with its inner quotes
% stripped is the desync defect this package screens for.) The `cites`
% corroboration states the SET and ORDER of the seven syllables — "commonly used
% in English-speaking countries: do …, re, mi, fa, so(l), la, and ti" (that quote
% carries a MARKED elision, `…` standing for "(spelt doh in tonic sol-fa),[2]",
% and it wraps across two `%` lines — a line-scoped screen cannot check it, which
% is a second desync axis recorded on #14111).
%
% Rows 3..7 are therefore a COMPOSITION of those two — the nth syllable of the
% cited order takes the nth degree — an INFERENCE, disclosed here and marked on
% each row below, NOT a verbatim claim of either span. A consumer reading only
% `citations` for `? solfege_degree(sol, $N)` gets spans that place `sol` in the
% syllable order but never call it the fifth degree. THE GAP IS THE DEGREE, not
% the syllable — the corroboration names `so(l)` outright, and the composition
% depends on it doing so. (`facts_solfege_e2e.rs` pins that exact answer, so the
% gap is visible in CI rather than only in prose.)
%
% The page DOES pair all seven, in a movable-do degree table — but that table's
% cells extract as separate blocks, which is exactly the cross-block class held
% open on #14111 ("is a table row a verbatim span?"), so it is deliberately not
% cited here rather than silently deciding that question. The canonical `sol`
% spelling is used for the atom. `trust consensus` — an encyclopedia is a
% secondary reference. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table solfege_degree {
    columns syllable, degree

    % STATED, verbatim, by the `source` sentence below:
    row (do, 1)     % page: The first degree of a major scale is always sung as "do"
    row (re, 2)     % page: …the second as "re", etc.

    % COMPOSED, not stated: the `source`'s "etc." continued through the syllable
    % ORDER the `cites` states. The citations these five rows carry place the
    % syllable in that order but never assign it a degree — see the header's
    % Provenance note.
    row (mi, 3)
    row (fa, 4)
    row (sol, 5)
    row (la, 6)
    row (ti, 7)

    source "In the movable do system, each solfège syllable corresponds not to a pitch, but to a scale degree: The first degree of a major scale is always sung as \"do\", the second as \"re\", etc."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solf%C3%A8ge"
    trust consensus

    % The syllable sentence stays as a corroboration rather than the envelope: it
    % states the SET and ORDER of the seven syllables, which is what it actually
    % says, while the `source` above states the degree RULE and its first two
    % terms. Pinning a degree to the list alone made "1" an ordinal INFERENCE
    % from list position, not a claim the citation makes; composing the two is
    % what carries the remaining five rows, and the header says so.
    cites "The tonic sol-fa method popularised the seven syllables commonly used in English-speaking countries: do (spelt doh in tonic sol-fa),[2] re, mi, fa, so(l), la, and ti (or si) (see below)." locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solf%C3%A8ge"
}
